Z||000020||Formula88||08-22-2001||12:06 AM||||Pretty!  I'm thinking the best way to do this would be go with the Weber carb setup and ditch the computer altogether.  All you really need to do is swap the distributor and a few small wiring mods (fuel pressure regulator, etc.)  It's been done with 4 barrel carbs, the only difference is the complexity of the Weber, which should give superior performance, but will be more difficult to tune.  Sure would look good under Skitime's decklid, though!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000021||artherd||08-22-2001||08:54 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B>Pretty!  I'm thinking the best way to do this would be go with the Weber carb setup and ditch the computer altogether.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No, it's not.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000022||Formula88||08-22-2001||11:08 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by artherd:<BR><B> No, it's not.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Care to explain that?  Do you know of a better way to put Weber carbs on a 2.8 V6?  <P>Carbureator conversions that remove the ECM have been done before.  This is just another type of carb conversion.  ||||reg||5
Z||000023||lowCG||08-22-2001||06:16 PM||||The way to do that nowadays is to use a programmable fuel injection ,say SDI,and one of the manifolds like in the photos above,with electronic injectors adapted.<BR>Webers were a true test of patience and skill,not just to maintain/synchronise,but to get jetted correctly;you could spend literally hundreds of dollars,and take up to a year to get the jets set-up.<BR>Jets;idle jet(fuel),idle jet(air)main jet,air corrector jet,emulsion tube,accelerator pump;spring+jet+bleedback(return),stroke.That's alot of variables that all have to work together.<BR>I feel that the high price of digital fuel control is very justified after wasting so much time myself that could've been spent doing so many other things. ||||reg||1
Z||000024||artherd||08-22-2001||07:17 PM||||Take a normal weber (already a PITA and complicated as hell) then multiply it by SIX.  Then get them all not only dialed in, but *matched*....<P>Yes, they have been done, and some people obviously like them, and that's cool, to each his own and all.... so long as I'm never downwind of your stinky pipes :)<P>IMHO however: Carbs are so damned **stupid** in this day and age, like carrying around a 800lb mainframe in your pocket to calculate figures on.  The 21st century is upon us any way you choose to look it, RIP carbs. Especially multi-carb setups.<P>I really can't adequately explain it, I hate carbs too much, but just try to tune a carb-per-cyl weber motorsport setup yourself one day(or year), and you'll want to hurt someone :)<P>lowCG: indeed, years (ok, months tops, but still) of my life I'll never have back :)<P>Multiple velocity stack EFI is bloody great in comparison, higher performance by any measure (including cost...) and I can set one up in anywhere from 5minuites to a day at the most. 3D fuel maps are duck soup, and they *work*<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lowCG:<BR><B><BR>Webers were a true test of patience and skill,not just to maintain/synchronise,but to get jetted correctly;you could spend literally hundreds of dollars,and take up to a year to get the jets set-up.<BR>Jets;idle jet(fuel),idle jet(air)main jet,air corrector jet,emulsion tube,accelerator pump;spring+jet+bleedback(return),stroke.That's alot of variables that all have to work together.<BR>I feel that the high price of digital fuel control is very justified after wasting so much time myself that could've been spent doing so many other things. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000025||Will||08-22-2001||09:11 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Slammed Fiero:<BR><B> I could swear that little metal butterfly type unit on a V6 with "Holley" writen on it is a Throttle body.<P><BR>In anycase...<P>try TWM induction , they built the setup for our 911 (6 TB's) looks like Webers /Deloroto's ...ect. Increased upper end power (Read massive gains) I Had thought of doing this several times. Use the lower and middle manifold. Then off of the middle manifold adapt your TB's , there are lots of various kinds out there , it's just a matter of finding the right equation for power. Would be a really nice setup , I think you would lose low end power , but the upper end gains would be well worth it.  Would be nice to see a hole in the decklid with 2 Box filters and 6 stacks poking out...<P>Could always go cheaper route and do 3 2 Downdraft webers. Be prepared to tune it quite often.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"><P>JM <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Per cylinder throttle bodies, if not accompanied by a commensurate increase in airflow capacity, <I>only</I> provide superior throttle response.<BR>The extra power comes from extra airflow.<BR>Using the stock Fiero intake manifolding with per-cylinder throttles is a waste of time as it will get you virtually nothing.||||reg||1
